window.docContents[98].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___15___-Representations-of-______mathfrak___sl____LPAR_2_RPAR____/Classification-of-finite-dimensional-representations-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___15___-Representations-of-______mathfrak___sl____LPAR_2_RPAR____--Classification-of-finite-dimensional-representations-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_--Classification-of-finite-dimensional-representations-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An irreducible module of rank one more than a natural number is nonemptily equivalent to the corresponding finite function module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary Lie homomorphism to endomorphisms from three endomorphisms satisfying the displayed bracket relations.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary Lie homomorphism sends the first displayed element to E.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary Lie homomorphism sends the second displayed element to F.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary Lie homomorphism sends the third displayed element to H.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nIn the representation indexed by lam + 1, the stated quadratic combination of three auxiliary elements is (lam * (lam + 2) / 2) times the identity.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe first auxiliary element sends the function at i to i times the function at i - 1.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nWhen i + 1 < d, the second auxiliary element sends the function at i to (d - 1 - i) times the function at i + 1.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe second auxiliary element sends the final indexed function to zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe third auxiliary element acts on the function at i by the scalar d - 1 - 2 * i.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nEvery displayed element is the stated linear combination of the three auxiliary elements using its matrix entries.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nFor nonzero d, the displayed Lie-module structure on complex-valued functions on Fin d is irreducible.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Theorem2.1.1; role=supporting]\n\nFor nonzero d, the complex vector space of functions on Fin d has finrank d.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nA finite-dimensional module over the displayed Lie algebra admits a Lie-module equivalence to a finite dependent product of complex coordinate-function spaces.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1/Derived10; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1/Derived11; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1/Derived12; role=supporting]\n\nThe distinguished endomorphism commutes with every Lie action.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nFor a finite-dimensional module, the generalized eigenspaces form an internal direct sum.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nEvery Lie submodule of a finite-dimensional module has a complementary Lie submodule.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1/Derived10; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1/Derived11; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1/Derived12; role=supporting]\n\nThere exists a linear equivalence intertwining the tensor-product endomorphism with the displayed direct-sum endomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nA sequence of complex polynomials indexed by natural numbers.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe polynomial indexed by k has natural degree k.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nOn a vector annihilated by the first displayed action, relates two iterated operators to polynomial evaluation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nEvery polynomial in the sequence is squarefree.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]\n\nExpands a product of two indexed polynomials as the displayed finite polynomial sum.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.15.1/Derived14; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.15. Representations of $\\mathfrak{sl}(2)$\u0009Classification of finite dimensional representations of sl(2)\u0009Classification of finite dimensional representations of sl(2)\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___15___-Representations-of-______mathfrak___sl____LPAR_2_RPAR____/Classification-of-finite-dimensional-representations-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___15___-Representations-of-______mathfrak___sl____LPAR_2_RPAR____--Classification-of-finite-dimensional-representations-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_--Classification-of-finite-dimensional-representations-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Transition-to-quiver-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Theorem2___1___1":{"contents":"As another example consider the representation theory of quivers. A quiver is an oriented graph Q (which we will assume to be finite). A representation of Q over a field k is an assignment of a k-vector space V_i to every vertex i of Q and of a linear operator A_h : V_i \\to V_j to every directed edge h going from i to j (loops and multiple edges are allowed). We will show that a representation of a quiver Q is the same thing as a representation of a certain algebra P_Q called the path algebra of Q. Thus one may ask: what are the indecomposable finite dimensional representations of Q?\n\nMore specifically, let us say that Q is of finite type if it has finitely many indecomposable representations.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.1. What is representation theory?\u0009Transition to quiver representation theory","header":"Transition to quiver representation theory","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Transition-to-quiver-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Theorem2___1___1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra--Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The canonical module structure on the singleton additive type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.3.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Examples of representations: zero, regular, k, free algebra\u0009Examples of representations: zero, regular, k, free algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra--Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Proof-of-Corollary-2___3___12/#Chapter2___Discussion_proof_Corollary2___3___12":{"contents":"Proof. Let V be irreducible. For any element a \\in A, the operator \\rho(a) : V \\to V is an intertwining operator. Indeed,\n\n\\rho(a)\\rho(b)v = \\rho(ab)v = \\rho(ba)v = \\rho(b)\\rho(a)v\n\n(the second equality is true since the algebra is commutative). Thus, by Schur's lemma, \\rho(a) is a scalar operator for any a \\in A. Hence every subspace of V is a subrepresentation. But V is irreducible, so 0 and V are the only subspaces of V. This means that \\dim V = 1 (since V \\neq 0). \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Proof of Corollary 2.3.12","header":"Proof of Corollary 2.3.12","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Proof-of-Corollary-2___3___12/#Chapter2___Discussion_proof_Corollary2___3___12"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Example-of-a-quiver/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Example-of-a-quiver--Example-of-a-quiver--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A quiver structure on the four-element type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.8.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe specified arrow from vertex zero to vertex one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.8.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe specified arrow from vertex two to vertex one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.8.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe specified arrow from vertex three to vertex zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.8.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Example of a quiver\u0009Example of a quiver\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Example-of-a-quiver/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Example-of-a-quiver--Example-of-a-quiver--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Notation-for-vertices-and-edges-of-quivers/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Notation-for-vertices-and-edges-of-quivers--Notation-for-vertices-and-edges-of-quivers--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The type of arrows in a quiver.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_quiver_notation; role=supporting]\n\nThe morphism underlying an arrow.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_quiver_notation; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Notation for vertices and edges of quivers\u0009Notation for vertices and edges of quivers\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Notation-for-vertices-and-edges-of-quivers/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Notation-for-vertices-and-edges-of-quivers--Notation-for-vertices-and-edges-of-quivers--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___10":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.10. Frobenius determinant\u0009Section 4.10: Frobenius determinant — group determinant setup","header":"Section 4.10: Frobenius determinant — group determinant setup","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___10"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___10___-Frobenius-determinant--Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup--Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: MvPolynomial.X\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter4/Introduction_4.10; role=supporting\n\nA multivariate polynomial whose variables are indexed by the elements of a finite group.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Definition4.10.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Introduction_4.10; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.10. Frobenius determinant\u0009Section 4.10: Frobenius determinant — group determinant setup\u0009Section 4.10: Frobenius determinant — group determinant setup\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___10___-Frobenius-determinant--Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup--Section-4___10___-Frobenius-determinant-___-group-determinant-setup--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/L_lambda-for-partitions-_LPAR_n_RPAR_-and-_LPAR_1___n_RPAR____-S___nV-and-______nV/#Chapter5___Example5___19___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.19. Schur-Weyl duality for $GL(V)$\u0009L_lambda for partitions (n) and (1^n): S^nV and Λ^nV\u0009L_lambda for partitions (n) and (1^n): S^nV and Λ^nV","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/L_lambda-for-partitions-_LPAR_n_RPAR_-and-_LPAR_1___n_RPAR____-S___nV-and-______nV/#Chapter5___Example5___19___3___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Enriched-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category--Enriched-categories--Enriched-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary type operator parameterized by a monoidal category.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Example7.1.5; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.1. The definition of a category\u0009Enriched categories\u0009Enriched categories\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Enriched-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category--Enriched-categories--Enriched-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Projective-cover/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___2___-Projective-covers--Projective-cover--Projective-cover--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The type attached to a given auxiliary object.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.2.2; role=supporting]\n\nAn opaque property asserted for the attached type over the ambient ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.2.2; role=supporting]\n\nIf a submodule together with the map kernel spans the attached type, then the submodule is\nall of it.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.2.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe additive commutative group structure provided on the attached type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.2.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe scalar action of the ambient ring on the attached type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.2.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe attached type is a projective module over the ambient ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.2.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe associated linear map reaches every element of the ambient module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.2.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe linear map from the attached type to the ambient module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.2.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.2. Projective covers\u0009Projective cover\u0009Projective cover\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Projective-cover/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___2___-Projective-covers--Projective-cover--Projective-cover--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-and-Cartan-matrix-of-path-algebras/#Chapter9___Problem9___4___6":{"contents":"Problem 9.4.6. (i) Show that the path algebra P_Q of any quiver Q with at least one edge has homological dimension 1. In particular, the homological dimension of the free algebra k\\langle x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\rangle is 1 (for n \\geq 1).\n\n(ii) Let Q be a finite oriented graph without oriented cycles. Find the Cartan matrix of its path algebra P_Q.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.4. Homological dimension\u0009Homological dimension and Cartan matrix of path algebras","header":"Homological dimension and Cartan matrix of path algebras","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-and-Cartan-matrix-of-path-algebras/#Chapter9___Problem9___4___6"}});